          %h3#some_details Some details
          %p
            Notice that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma : \emptyset \to \partial \Sigma
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z(\Sigma) : 1 \to Z(\partial \Sigma)
                  \\,,
          %p
            hence an element of the space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(\partial \Sigma)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Sigma
          %p
            The idea that some systems in physics are governed by other systems “localized at a boundary” in this kind of way was originally suggested by the behaviour of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes"} black holes
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole+entropy"} black hole entropy
            is proportional to their “surface”, as reflected by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+second+law+of+thermodynamics"} generalized second law of thermodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%27t+Hooft"} Gerard 't Hooft
            suggest a general principle, called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em holographic principle
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#tHooft93"} t’Hooft 93
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Susskind94"} Susskind 94
          %p Later, two more precise classes of correspondences were identified, that are regarded now as precise examples of the general idea of the holographic principle:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Systems of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
                can be shown explicitly to have spaces of states that are canonically identified with correlator spaces of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
                = succeed "s)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+block"} conformal block
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
                on their boundary.
              %p
                (The relation of traditional 3d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                to the 2d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
                originates in (
                %a{:href => "#Witten89"}> Witten 89
                ) and hence precedes the proposal of (
                %a{:href => "#tHooft93"}> t’Hooft 93
                ,
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "#Susskind94"} Susskind 94
            %li
              %p
                Systems of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
                in various dimensions as given by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
                on asymptotically
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric"} supersymmetric
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              In view of these two classes of examples it is maybe noteworthy that one can see that also closed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"} L-infinity algebra
              of closed string correlators. So maybe the above two different realizations of the holographic principle are really aspects of one single mechanism for
              = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %p
              Evidence for this also comes from the details of the AdS/CFT mechanism. In (
              %a{:href => "#Witten98"}> Witten98
              ) it is discussed how the
              %a{:href => "#SYMAds5"} SYM/IIB duality
              is carried by the Chern-Simons term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∫
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi NS
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi RR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int B_{NS} \wedge d B_{RR}
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
              action, the
              %a{:href => "#6dAdS7"} 6d(2,0)/AdS7 duality
              \- is induced by the Chern-Simons term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∫
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int C_3 \wedge d C_3 \wedge d C_3
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
              action.
          %p
            Below at
            %em
              %a{:href => "Examples"} Examples
            we list some systems for which something along these lines is known.
          %h3#MoreDetails More details
          %p We discuss in a bit more detail the central idea of holography, roughly for the case of Chern-Simons type theories and making some simplifications, but giving a precise statement.
          %p
            The general idea is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            in the bulk theory are identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source+fields"} source fields
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+functions"} correlation functions
            of the boundary theory.
          %p
            The archetypical example is the relation between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            of 3d
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Gawedzki99"} Gawędzki 99
          %p
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} correlator
            for the WZW model with source field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has to satisfy a conformal transformation property called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ward+identity"} Ward identity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+functions"} wave functions
            of Chern-Simons theory depending on the fields
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            More generally, consider some
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_B
            and assume that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
            that it assigns to any
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are of finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            (over some ground field
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn ∞
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  dim Z_B(X) \lt \infty
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi in
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi out
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma : \partial_{in}{\Sigma} \to \partial_{out}{\Sigma}
            any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            of dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} correlator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi in
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi out
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z_B(\Sigma) : Z_B(\partial_{in} \Sigma) \to Z_B(\partial_{out} \Sigma)
          %p
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_B
            assigns may naturally be identified, under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal structure
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi Z
                                %mi B
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi Σ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ¯
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi Z
                            %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mo ∂
                            %mi in
                          %mi Σ
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo *
                          %mo ⊗
                          %msub
                            %mi Z
                            %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mo ∂
                            %mi out
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                          %msub
                            %mi Z
                            %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\overline{Z_B(\Sigma)}
                  \&amp; \in
                  Z_B(\partial_{in} \Sigma)^{*} \otimes Z_B(\partial_{out} \Sigma)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \simeq
                  Z_B(\partial \Sigma)
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Stated differently: the vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_B(\partial \Sigma)
            is the space of all “potential correlators” of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{Z_B(\Sigma)}
            is the particular one chosen by the given model.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_B
            is really a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            one calls a subspace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bl
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bl_B(\Sigma) \subset Z(\partial\Sigma)
            of elements that respect conformal invariance in a certain way the space of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+block"}> conformal block
              s
            and calls the assignment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi Bl
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \mapsto Bl_B(\Sigma)
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
            of the model.
          %p
            Notice that by looking at all “potential correlators” this way we are suddenly assigning vector spaces in codimension 0 (on
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            a priori only mention vector spaces (of states) assigned in codimension 1. Given all these spaces of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+block"}> conformal block
            s”, the (re)construction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_B
            consists of choosing inside each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bl
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bl_B(\Sigma)
            the actual correlator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{Z_B(\Sigma)}
            (this way of looking at
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is actually the way in which
            %a{:href => ""} Atiyah
            originally formuated the axioms of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %p
            But since we are dealing now with vector spaces assigned to
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            is there an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"} extended TQFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            such that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                there is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Bl
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Z_A(\Sigma) \simeq Z_B(\partial \Sigma) = Bl_B(\Sigma)
            %li
              %p
                such that whenever
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \Sigma
                cobounds
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                the linear map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∂
                  %mover
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Bl
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z_A : \mathbb{C} = Z_A(\hat \Sigma)
                  \\stackrel{Z_A(\hat \Sigma)}{\to}
                  Z_A(\partial \hat \Sigma)
                  \\simeq
                  Bl_B(\Sigma)
          %p
            sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in \mathbb{C}
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Z
                          %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{Z_B(\Sigma)}
          %p
            If so, we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %strong holographic dual
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            Notice that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_A(\Sigma)
            is the space of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"}> state
              s
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bl
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bl_B(\Sigma)
            is the space of possible
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"}> correlator
              s
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            are identified with the correlators of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#PoissonHolography Poisson holography
          %p
            One of the key statements of the holographic principle is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk+field+theory"} bulk field theory
            correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sources"} sources
            in its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
          %p
            One set-up where this can be made a formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            is for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            which is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/off-shell+Poisson+bracket#BoundaryFieldTheoryInterpretation"} off-shell Poisson bracket – boundary field theory interpretation
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "motivic+quantization#PoissonHolography"} motivic quantization – Poisson holography
                \.
          %h3#3dCS-2dCFT Holography of higher Chern-Simons/CFT-type
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
          %h4#rt3d_tqft__rational_2d_cft RT-3d TQFT / rational 2d CFT
          %p
            The class of examples of “Chern-Simons-type holography” we mention now has fairly completely and rigorously been understood. It is in turn a special and comparatively simple (but far from trivial) case of the historically earliest class of examples:
            %a{:href => "#OrdinaryCSWZWModel"} ordinary Chern-Simons theory dual to a 2d WZW model
            below.
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
          %p
            Given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
            produces a 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_C
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+block"} conformal block
            = succeed "-liked" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
            CFT. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS+formalism"} FRS formalism
            provides a way to show that the states of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_C
            provides
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            that solve the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sewing+constraints"} sewing constraints
          %h4#OrdinaryCSWZWModel Ordinary Chern-Simons theory / WZW-model
          %p
            For a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            for a group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is holographically dual to the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
            describing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            propagating on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p Here is a list with aspects of this correspondence:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                At the level of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
                the relation is directly seen by observing that on a 3-d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"} manifold with boundary
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                action is not gauge invariant, but has a boundary term depending on the gauge transformation. Since the gauge transformation is a function on the 2d boundary with values in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
              %p
                A random source reviewing this is for instance (
                %a{:href => "#ArcioniBlauLoughlin"}> Arcioni-Blau-Loughlin, p. 6
                ).
            %li
              %p
                More abstractly, at least for simply connected compact
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
                as discussed at
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
                = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi c
                        %mi conn
                      %mo :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi conn
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mn 3
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi conn
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathbf{c}_{conn} : \mathbf{B}G_{conn} \to \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)_{conn}
              %p
                from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
                of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} bundles with connection
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+infinity-stack"} moduli 3-stack
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle 3-bundles with connection
                (discussed in detail at
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+string+structure"} differential string structure
                ) in that for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_3
                a compact 3d-dimensional surface the Chern-Simons action is the composite
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi conn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msub
                            %mi Σ
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi c
                            %mi conn
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mn 3
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi conn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi Σ
                                %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\exp(i S_{CS}(-)) : [\Sigma_3, \mathbf{B} G_{conn}] \stackrel{[\Sigma_3, \mathbf{c}_{conn}]}{\to} [\Sigma_3, \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)_{conn}] \stackrel{\exp(2 \pi i \int_{\Sigma_3}(-))}{\to} U(1)
                      \\,,
              %p
                where the last morphism is given by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+ordinary+differential+cohomology"} fiber integration in ordinary differential cohomology
              %p
                Topological term in the WZW-model (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
                2. This is discussed at
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "Chern-Simons%20theory#GeometricQuantHigher"} Chern-Simons theory – Geometric quantization – In higher codimension
            %li
              %p
                At the level of matching
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states"} space of states
                of CS-theory with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
                of the WZW model this is a computation obtained from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
                of the CS-action, originally due to (
                %a{:href => "#Witten"}> Witten
                ). A review is in (
                %a{:href => "#Gawedzki"}> Gawedzki, section 5
                ).
            %li
              %p
                If one accepts that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
                of the
                = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                yields the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
                given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
                applied to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
                of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+group"} loop group
                representations, then a detailed construction of the correspondence CS-TQFT/WZW-CFT is what the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FFRS-formalism
                achieves. See there for more details.
              %p
                More comments on the holographic interpretation of this formalism are in (
                %a{:href => "#KapustinSaulina"}> Kapustin-Saulina
                ,
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#FuchsSchweigertValentino"} Fuchs-Schweigert-Valentino
          %h4#poisson_model__quantum_mechanics
            Poisson
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            Ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            induced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            – which may be regarded as a 1-dimensional QFT – is holographically dual to the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            (implicitly observed by (
            %a{:href => "#Kontsevich"}> Kontsevich
            ) made explicit by (
            %a{:href => "#CattaneoFelder"}> CattaneoFelder
            ).
          %p
            (Notice the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            is the
            = succeed "-case" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n = 2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+sigma-model"} AKSZ sigma-model
            which is indeed an example of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
          %h4#amodel__quantum_mechanics A-model / quantum mechanics
          %p
            Similarly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            on certain
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#WittenAModel"} Witten
          %p See
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+via+the+A-model"} quantization via the A-model
          %p
            Notice that the A-model arises from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
          %h4#HigherDimCSAndSelfDualQFT Higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory / Self-dual higher gauge theory
          %h5#idea_and_examples Idea and examples
          %p
            Generally,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
            (for
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+2
            (at least in the abelian case).
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k=0)
                \: ordinary 3-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                is related to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
                on its boundary;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k=1)
                \: 7-dimensional Chern-Simons theory is related to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane"} fivebrane
                model on its boundary;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k=2)
                \: 11-dimensional Chern-Simons theory is related to a parts of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
                on its boundary (or that of the space-filling 9-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"}> brane
                , if one wishes) (
                %a{:href => "#BelovMoore"}> BelovMoore
                ).
          %h5#some_details_2 Some details
          %p
            We indicate why
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            is – if holographically related to anything – holographically related to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \times \mathbb{R}
            can be identified with the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} flat
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2k+1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+form"} presymplectic form
            on this space is given by the pairing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi δ
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mi δ
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi δ
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\delta B_1, \delta B_2) \mapsto
                  \\int_\Sigma \delta B_1 \wedge \delta B_2
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of the theory requires that we choose a polarization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexification"} complexification
            of this space (split the space of forms into “coordinates” and their “canonical momenta”).
          %p
            One way to achieve this is to choose a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+structure"} conformal structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\star : \Omega^{2k+1}(\Sigma) \to \Omega^{2k+1}(\Sigma)
          %p provides the polarization by splitting into self-dual and anti-self-dual forms:
          %p
            notice that (by the formulas at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 4
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\star \star B = (-1)^{(2k+1)(4k+3)} B
                  \= - B
                  \\,.
          %p
            Therefore it provides a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{2k+1}(\Sigma) \otimes \mathbb{C}
          %p We see that the symplectic structure on the space of forms can equivalently be rewritten as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mo ⋆
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\int_X B_1 \wedge B_2
                  \&amp; = - \int_X B_1 \wedge \star \star B_2
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here on the right now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge inner product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star B_2
            appears, which is invariant under applying the Hodge star to both arguments.
          %p
            We then decompose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{2k+1}(\Sigma)
            into the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm i
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenspace"} eigenspace
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \in \Omega^{2k+1}(\Sigma)
            is
            %em imaginary self-dual
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\star B = i B
          %p
            and
            %em imaginary anti-self-dual
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi i
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\star B = - i B
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then for imaginary self-dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
            we find that the symplectic pairing is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mo ⋆
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo ⋆
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∧
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo ⋆
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mo ⋆
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  (B_1, B_2)
                  \&amp;=
                  \-i \int_X B_1 \wedge \star B_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \-i \int_X (\star B_1) \wedge \star (\star B_2)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  +i \int_X B_1 \wedge \star B_2
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Therefore indeed the symplectic pairing vanishes on the self-dual and on the anti-selfdual forms. Evidently these provide a decomposition into
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+subspace"} Lagrangian subspace
          %p
            Therefore a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            of higher Chern-Simons theory on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            may locally be thought of as a function of the self-dual forms on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} correlator
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %h3#holography_of_ads_gravitycfttype Holography of AdS gravity/CFT-type
          %h4#SYMAds5
            Type II on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 5
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_5 \times S^5
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4
            super Yang-Mills
          %p
            Conjecturally,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-de+Sitter+space"} anti-de Sitter space
            background is holographically dual to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            on the asymptotic boundary.
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
          %h4#6dAdS7
            M-theory on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 7
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_7 \times S^4
            and 6d
            = succeed "-SCFT" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,0)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} M-theory
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 7
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_7 \times S^4
            is supposed to have as holographic boundary the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
          %h4#mtheory_on__and_chernsimons_on_m2_branes
            M-theory on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 7
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_4 \times S^7/\mathbb{Z}_k
            and Chern-Simons on M2 branes
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ABJM+theory"} ABJM theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+entanglement+entropy"} holographic entanglement entropy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle+of+higher+category+theory"} holographic principle of higher category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/firewall+problem"} firewall problem
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The idea of the holographic principle in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            originates with:
          %ul
            %li#tHooft93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%27t+Hooft"}> Gerard 't Hooft
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dimensional Reduction in Quantum Gravity
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/conferences/968293"} Conference on Highlights of Particle and Condensed Matter Physics (SALAMFEST)
                (1993) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/9310026"}> gr-qc/9310026
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/36137"} spire:36137
            %li#Susskind94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leonard+Susskind"}> Leonard Susskind
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The World as a hologram
                %strong 36
                (1995) 6377-6396 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9409089"}> hep-th/9409089
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.531249"} doi:10.1063/1.531249
          %p
            Early suggestion of holography in the form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
            being described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            in a higher dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            (later discussed as
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-QCD+duality"} AdS-QCD duality
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em String Theory and Quark Confinement
                %strong 68
                (1998) 1-8 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9711002"}> arXiv:hep-th/9711002
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0920-5632(98)00135-2"} doi:10.1016/S0920-5632(98)00135-2
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The wall of the cave
                %strong 14
                (1999) 645-658 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9809057"}> arXiv:hep-th/9809057
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217751X99000324"} doi:10.1142/S0217751X99000324
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              R. Bousso,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The holographic principle
              %strong 74
              (2002) 825-874 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1925130"}> MR2003m:83048
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/RevModPhys.74.825"} doi
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0203101"} arXiv:hep-th/0203101
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/AndreiStarinets/oxford_holography_group/holography_seminar/group_members.html"}> Oxford Holography Group
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/AndreiStarinets/oxford_holography_group/holography_seminar/material.html"} Background material for holography
          %h3#adscft AdS/CFT
          %p
            See the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
          %h3#ReferencesCS-CFT Chern-Simons / CFT
          %h4#on_the_level_of_action_functionals On the level of action functionals
          %p
            The identification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            of 3d
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            with the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Witten89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum Field Theory and the Jones Polynomial
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104178138"} euclid:cmp/1104178138
          %p
            A review of the standard holographic relation between 3d
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is for instance around p. 30 of
          %ul
            %li#Gawedzki99
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Krzysztof+Gaw%C4%99dzki"}> Krzysztof Gawędzki
              ,
              %em Conformal field theory: a case study
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9904145"}> arXiv:hep-th/9904145
              )
          %p
            Discussion of how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            reproduce overe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundaries"} boundaries
            the action functional of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            are for instance on p. 6 of
          %ul#ArcioniBlauLoughlin
            %li
              Giovanni Arcioni,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Blau"} Matthias Blau
              %em On the boundary dynamics of Chern-Simons gravity
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0210089"}> arXiv:0210089
              )
          %p (And many other references. )
          %h4#matching_of_spaces_of_states_to_conformal_blocks Matching of spaces of states to conformal blocks
          %p
            The observation that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states"} space of states
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            matches with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            is originally due to
          %ul#Witten
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              %em Quantum Field Theory and the Jones Polynomial
              Commun. Math. Phys. 121 (3) (1989) 351–399. MR0990772 (
              %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104178138"}> project EUCLID
              )
          %p A review is in section 5 of
          %ul#Gawedzki
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Krzysztof+Gawedzki"}> Krzysztof Gawedzki
              ,
              %em Conformal field theory: a case study
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9904145"}> arXiv:hep-th/9904145
              )
          %h4#RTAnd2dCFT Reshetikhin-Turaev 3d TQFT and rational 2d CFT
          %p
            Using the hypothesized relation between
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            to that given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
            applied to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+group"} loop group
            representations, a detailed discussion of the relation CS/WZW in given by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS+formalism"} FFRS formalism
          %p One article that contains a survey of much of the story is
          %ul
            %li
              Jens Fjelstad, Jürgen Fuchs,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ingo+Runkel"} Ingo Runkel
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Schweigert"} Christoph Schweigert
              %em Uniqueness of open/closed rational CFT with given algebra of open states
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0612306"}> arXiv:hep-th/0612306
              ) .
          %p The isomorphism between the RT-theory modular functor and the CFT conformal blocks is also discussed in
          %ul
            %li#AndersenUeno
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%B8rgen+Andersen"}> Jørgen Andersen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenji+Ueno"} Kenji Ueno
              %em Construction of the Reshetikhin-Turaev TQFT from conformal field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5027"}> arXiv:1110.5027
              )
          %p
            Amplification of how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS+formalism"} FRS formalism
            is inevitable once one adopts holography and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} QFT with defects
            is in
          %ul#KapustinSaulina
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anton+Kapustin"}> Anton Kapustin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Natalia+Saulina"} Natalia Saulina
              %em Surface operators in 3d TFT and 2d Rational CFT
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              (eds.)
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
              AMS, 2011
          %p More along these lines is in
          %ul#FuchsSchweigertValentino
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%BCrgen+Fuchs"}> Jürgen Fuchs
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Schweigert"} Christoph Schweigert
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Valentino"} Alessandro Valentino
              %em Bicategories for boundary conditions and for surface defects in 3-d TFT
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1203.4568"}> arXiv:1203.4568
              )
          %h4#selfdual_higher_gauge_fields_and_higher_abelian_chernsimons Self-dual higher gauge fields and higher abelian Chern-Simons
          %p
            The idea of describing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            by abelian Chern-Simons theory in one dimension higher originates in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Five-brane effective action in M-Theory
                %strong 22
                (1997), no. 2, 103–133,
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9610234"} hep-th/9610234
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Duality relations among topological effects in string theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912086"} arXiv:hep-th/9912086
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1088/1126-6708/2000/05/031"} doi
          %p More discussion of the general principle is in
          %ul#BelovMoore
            %li
              Dmitriy Belov,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"} Greg Moore
              = succeed "," do
                %em Holographic action for the self-dual field
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0605038"} arXiv:hep-th/0605038
          %p A quick exposition of the basic idea is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Distler"}> Jacques Distler
              ,
              %em Actions for self-dual gauge fields
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/~distler/blog/archives/000809.html"}> blog
              )
          %p
            The application of this to the description of type II
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            in 10-dimensions to 11-dimensional Chern-Simons theory is in the followup
          %ul
            %li
              Dmitriy Belov,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"} Greg Moore
              %em Type II Actions from 11-Dimensional Chern-Simons Theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0611020"}> arXiv
              )
          %h4#poisson_modelamodel_and_quantum_mechanics
            Poisson
            = succeed "-model/A-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %ul#Kontsevich
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              , …
          %ul#CattaneoFelder
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Felder"} Giovanni Felder
          %ul#WittenAModel
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              , …
          %h4#3d_chernsimons_theory__2d_cft 3d Chern-Simons theory / 2d CFT
          %p
            3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            in the context of holography is discussed for instance in
          %ul
            %li
              Victor O. Rivelles,
              %em Holographic Principle and AdS/CFT Correspondence
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912139"}> arXiv
              )
          %h4#chernsimonscft_in_adscft Chern-Simons/CFT in AdS/CFT
          %p In
          %ul#Witten98
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              %em AdS/CFT Correspondence And Topological Field Theory
              JHEP 9812:012,1998 (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9812012"}> arXiv:hep-th/9812012
              )
          %p
            it is argued that in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
            it is in fact just the Chern-Simon terms inside the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theories whose states control the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+block"} conformal block
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi CFT
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_5/CFT_4
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi AdS
                      %mn 7
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi CFT
                      %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_7/CFT_6
          %h3#black_hole__cft_correspondence Black hole / CFT correspondence
          %p
            For
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kerr+black+holes"} Kerr black holes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Monica+Guica"}> Monica Guica
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Hartman"} Thomas Hartman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Strominger"} Andrew Strominger
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Kerr/CFT Correspondence
                %strong 80
                (2009) 124008 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4266"}> arXiv:0809.4266
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.80.124008"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.80.124008
            %li
              %p
                Alejandra Castro, Alexander Maloney,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Strominger"} Andrew Strominger
                %em Hidden Conformal Symmetry of the Kerr Black Hole
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1004.0996"}> arXiv:1004.0996
                )
          %h3#general_abstract_formulation General abstract formulation
          %p
            An identification of boundary conditions and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} defects
            as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %ul#SchommerPries
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"}> Chris Schommer-Pries
              ,
              %em Topological defects and classifying local topological field theories in low dimension
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SchommerPriesDefects.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle+of+higher+category+theory"} holographic principle of higher category theory
            for more on this.
          %p
            Further discussion of formalization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"} extended TQFT
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-3-2+8-7-6"} 4-3-2 8-7-6
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/tillmann/ASPECTS.html"} ASPECTS of Topology
              Dec 2012
        .revisedby
